WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:01.650
- My name is unusual, Kirsten,

00:00:01.650 --> 00:00:02.820
you don't see it very often,

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:05.603
especially my age and younger.

00:00:05.603 --> 00:00:08.250
And one day, I was
wandering around Seaworld

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:09.810
and I went through the food line,

00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:12.520
and the girl working the cash
register was named Kirsten.

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:14.660
So I asked her how she got her name,

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:16.503
and she said that the house she grew up on

00:00:16.503 --> 00:00:18.747
was called Kirsten Place,

00:00:18.747 --> 00:00:20.610
and her parents just liked the name

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:22.380
so they named her after the street.

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:24.330
But what's really funny about the story is

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:26.770
my dad is the one who named that street.

00:00:26.770 --> 00:00:29.140
He named two streets in Spring Valley,

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:33.130
Karen Lane after my sister
and Kirsten Place after me,

00:00:33.130 --> 00:00:34.470
because he was a developer

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:37.393
and he got to name the
streets in that town.

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:43.590
- I was actually abroad in New Zealand

00:00:43.590 --> 00:00:45.190
and I was talking to a stranger about how

00:00:45.190 --> 00:00:47.139
I wanted to go to a
hiking trail further off,

00:00:47.139 --> 00:00:49.851
and she gave me her car for the day.

00:00:49.851 --> 00:00:52.100
Just because I wanted
to go to a hiking trail

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:52.933
that was far away.

00:00:52.933 --> 00:00:54.230
She didn't even know if I could drive,

00:00:54.230 --> 00:00:55.380
let alone who I was,

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:57.200
and just gave me her car.

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:00.350
So I definitely remember
her, she was incredibly kind.

00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:02.480
- So once, many years ago

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:05.250
when I was a student in
Milwaukee, Wisconsin,

00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:08.730
I was crossing a street near my apartment

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:10.422
in a not so great area,

00:01:10.422 --> 00:01:15.020
and a man approached me and took my scarf

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:18.240
and squeezed it across my neck

00:01:20.298 --> 00:01:22.230
in an effort to attack me.

00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:27.230
And I was frightened, and I
fought him off and ran home.

00:01:30.211 --> 00:01:32.650
I've never forgotten him.

00:01:32.650 --> 00:01:33.570
- [Girl] What were the feelings

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:35.070
you were left with after that?

00:01:36.575 --> 00:01:40.673
- A state of vulnerability that
I'd not experienced before.

00:01:41.627 --> 00:01:45.537
- The one stranger I remember
was in a grocery store,

00:01:45.537 --> 00:01:46.810
the one in front of me,

00:01:46.810 --> 00:01:48.420
noticed that we didn't have
money to pay for our food,

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:50.240
so she paid for all of our food.

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:51.870
It was over 100 dollars.

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:53.790
And I didn't know her name or anything,

00:01:53.790 --> 00:01:55.870
she just paid for
everything and walked away.

00:01:55.870 --> 00:01:58.544
I've never seen her again,
but she stays in my head

00:01:58.544 --> 00:02:01.830
and I thank God for her
because she did a lot, a lot,

00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:03.220
when we needed her.

00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:06.950
- I remember back in my
hometown in East Texas,

00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:08.920
this stranger randomly ran up to me,

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:10.190
without saying a word,

00:02:10.190 --> 00:02:11.870
and to this day I still
don't know his name,

00:02:11.870 --> 00:02:13.440
ran up to me, handed me 20 dollars

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:16.181
and took off in the opposite direction.

00:02:16.181 --> 00:02:18.390
(laughing)

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:22.040
Nobody came running up
to it, so I kept it.

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:24.890
- It was Fourth of July, 1999,

00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:26.490
the Starbucks in Coronado,

00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:28.430
sitting, enjoying some tea,

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:29.800
obviously kind of looking around

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:31.460
to see if there are any beautiful women.

00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:32.513
In walks a beautiful woman,

00:02:32.513 --> 00:02:34.090
with her parents, no less,

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:38.003
and I found a way to start
a conversation with her,

00:02:38.003 --> 00:02:42.223
and nearly 20 years later,
we're still together.

00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:52.195
- I met a stranger in an
airport who's a retired nurse

00:02:52.195 --> 00:02:56.550
that had saved about 80
children from a plane crash

00:02:56.550 --> 00:02:58.750
and that left an indelible episode.

00:02:58.750 --> 00:03:02.441
She was a young nurse, a
lieutenant in the Air Force,

00:03:02.441 --> 00:03:07.441
and she was evacuating
Amerasian children from Vietnam

00:03:07.521 --> 00:03:11.130
at the fall of the capital,

00:03:11.130 --> 00:03:13.028
and the plane crashed on takeoff

00:03:13.028 --> 00:03:15.640
and she saved the children.

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:18.690
No matter how badly injured she was,

00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:20.490
she put up with the pain

00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:22.980
to rescue those who were dependent on her,

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:26.000
and that made a lot of difference for me.

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:29.470
- So I was on a cruise
and I was just sitting,

00:03:29.470 --> 00:03:30.303
looking at the view,

00:03:30.303 --> 00:03:32.820
I had lost my family, I
didn't know where they were,

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:33.930
so I was just trying to

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:36.530
watch the ocean and watch everything,

00:03:36.530 --> 00:03:39.655
and I was at a very
confused point in my life,

00:03:39.655 --> 00:03:42.980
not really knowing what
I wanted to do with it.

00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:45.850
And this random lady just came up to me

00:03:45.850 --> 00:03:48.463
with her bottle of wine
and a book in her hand,

00:03:48.463 --> 00:03:51.890
and she just starts talking
to me about her life,

00:03:51.890 --> 00:03:53.030
and she was telling me about how

00:03:53.030 --> 00:03:54.924
she was a nurse in Spain

00:03:54.924 --> 00:03:57.730
and she was doing public health

00:03:57.730 --> 00:04:00.290
and teaching moms how to breastfeed

00:04:00.290 --> 00:04:02.116
and take care of their babies.

00:04:02.116 --> 00:04:04.100
And I didn't know her name,

00:04:04.100 --> 00:04:05.570
she didn't tell me anything about her,

00:04:05.570 --> 00:04:07.550
she was just very happy and relaxed

00:04:07.550 --> 00:04:10.730
and she just walked away and
told me to have a nice day

00:04:10.730 --> 00:04:12.630
and that was one time when I knew

00:04:12.630 --> 00:04:14.120
that I wanted to do nursing,

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:15.800
so that was pretty cool.

00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:17.150
- [Girl] So you're
studying nursing right now?

00:04:17.150 --> 00:04:18.860
- Yep, that's what I'm
doing right now, yeah.

00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:19.850
- That's crazy!

00:04:19.850 --> 00:04:21.600
- Yeah, yeah, it's really cool.

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:22.620
- I was ice skating

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:24.344
and there was a little five year old girl,

00:04:24.344 --> 00:04:26.344
she walked up to me and she goes,

00:04:26.344 --> 00:04:28.347
"Can I just tell you something?

00:04:28.347 --> 00:04:30.750
"You are the most beautiful
girl I've seen today."

00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:32.632
And it literally made my entire month,

00:04:32.632 --> 00:04:35.950
I'd never gotten a compliment like that

00:04:35.950 --> 00:04:37.530
from someone so little,

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:39.730
and I didn't think it would matter,

00:04:39.730 --> 00:04:41.593
I don't know, it just did.

00:04:41.593 --> 00:04:42.426
- So many years ago

00:04:42.426 --> 00:04:44.190
I was coming back from a
camping trip with my family,

00:04:44.190 --> 00:04:45.380
and this was pretty late at night

00:04:45.380 --> 00:04:46.680
so I was sleeping in the backseat

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:47.953
and my brother was there as well,

00:04:47.953 --> 00:04:51.024
and for some reason which I
don't understand to this day,

00:04:51.024 --> 00:04:53.180
the door opened at the back

00:04:53.180 --> 00:04:54.610
and I actually flew out

00:04:54.610 --> 00:04:56.560
while we were on the middle of the road,

00:04:56.560 --> 00:04:59.414
going at probably 40 to 50 miles an hour.

00:04:59.414 --> 00:05:01.370
So I landed at the road,

00:05:01.370 --> 00:05:02.760
but at this time I'm still sleeping,

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:04.950
I'm just like, "What just happened?"

00:05:04.950 --> 00:05:06.360
and my parents just drove ahead

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:08.830
because they didn't notice
until maybe five minutes later,

00:05:08.830 --> 00:05:10.500
where my mom heard the door

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:12.330
making a "woosh" sound at the back,

00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:13.207
and looked back and she's like,

00:05:13.207 --> 00:05:14.320
"Oh my god, where's Anan?"

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:15.817
They went to the side
and stopped immediately,

00:05:15.817 --> 00:05:17.680
and at this point they're not even sure

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:19.340
where I am, what happened,

00:05:19.340 --> 00:05:23.350
but luckily enough, someone
who was driving up stopped

00:05:23.350 --> 00:05:24.840
because they saw something
in the middle of the road

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:26.235
that resembled a person,

00:05:26.235 --> 00:05:28.589
and they got out of the
car, helped me to the side,

00:05:28.589 --> 00:05:31.740
and I'm completely lost
and crying at this point,

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:33.730
and they walked me back
while my parents walked back,

00:05:33.730 --> 00:05:35.274
so they kind of met in the middle.

00:05:35.274 --> 00:05:37.333
And yeah, I don't remember his face,

00:05:37.333 --> 00:05:39.088
but for obvious reasons

00:05:39.088 --> 00:05:41.940
that's someone who's
basically changed my life,

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:44.980
because I wouldn't be here,
per se, if they ran over me.

00:05:44.980 --> 00:05:47.050
Yeah, my parents said thank
you to them in the newspaper

00:05:47.050 --> 00:05:47.883
and stuff like that,

00:05:47.883 --> 00:05:50.320
so some pretty cool and crazy stuff.

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:54.164
- So I had someone message me
on Instagram a long time ago,

00:05:54.164 --> 00:05:56.166
and we kind of befriended each other.

00:05:56.166 --> 00:05:58.703
And then maybe a year later

00:05:58.703 --> 00:06:01.260
they randomly messaged me and asked me

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:02.810
if I wanted to go to the Bahamas.

00:06:02.810 --> 00:06:04.900
And I was like, well,
that's kind of weird,

00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:09.900
but it was for a competition
that he won for Tinder

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:13.690
to where we would go on our first date

00:06:13.690 --> 00:06:17.040
to swim with sharks for
the Discovery Channel,

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:19.620
so I met a complete stranger in Florida

00:06:19.620 --> 00:06:21.180
and we flew with a bunch of people

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:24.080
and went to the Bahamas
to swim with sharks,

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:25.990
and that was the first time I met him.

00:06:25.990 --> 00:06:28.290
- I went to hike with my parents,

00:06:28.290 --> 00:06:32.450
it was really hot outside and
we forgot our water bottles

00:06:32.450 --> 00:06:34.370
and he was pretty, pretty, pretty old

00:06:34.370 --> 00:06:35.880
and was sitting on a bench,

00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:38.030
and I was pretty dehydrated.

00:06:38.030 --> 00:06:41.670
And he was getting some
water out of the well

00:06:41.670 --> 00:06:43.050
and putting it in his hands

00:06:43.050 --> 00:06:45.870
and he gave it to me
in his hands to drink.

00:06:45.870 --> 00:06:48.680
And I was so relieved
that I can get some water,

00:06:48.680 --> 00:06:50.090
I didn't even speak his language,

00:06:50.090 --> 00:06:51.589
he was Greek or something,

00:06:51.589 --> 00:06:55.467
and he gave us all water,

00:06:55.467 --> 00:06:59.879
it was really nice and I
remember it to this day.

00:06:59.879 --> 00:07:02.216
- [Girl] So you all drank
water out of his hands?

00:07:02.216 --> 00:07:03.466
- Yeah, we did.

00:07:04.470 --> 00:07:08.220
- Actually a gentleman I
met about six months ago,

00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:12.310
I guess you could say homeless,
or just not doing too well,

00:07:12.310 --> 00:07:14.410
we just had a great conversation

00:07:14.410 --> 00:07:16.010
for about half an hour, an hour.

00:07:16.970 --> 00:07:20.130
It's honestly the most frequent memory

00:07:20.130 --> 00:07:22.460
that I think about, every now and again.

00:07:22.460 --> 00:07:24.840
- [Girl] Do you remember
what you guys talked about?

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:25.840
- Family mostly.

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:28.497
He told me about his kids
and how he missed them,

00:07:28.497 --> 00:07:31.080
and how he wished he
could do more for 'em,

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:32.210
and I was telling him about

00:07:32.210 --> 00:07:34.780
how I wished I had a
family like that one day,

00:07:34.780 --> 00:07:39.343
and being able to raise my
kids in a healthy home, really.

00:07:40.210 --> 00:07:45.210
Just, even given where he
was, he seemed as inspiring

00:07:46.533 --> 00:07:49.310
as many of the people that I know today

00:07:49.310 --> 00:07:52.216
who are doing much better
in life than even I am,

00:07:52.216 --> 00:07:53.490
to be honest,

00:07:53.490 --> 00:07:54.960
and he saw life in a way

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:57.033
that people don't truly
understand it anymore,

00:07:57.033 --> 00:07:59.630
which is, everyone is equal.

00:07:59.630 --> 00:08:01.390
- I remember a woman coming up to me,

00:08:01.390 --> 00:08:03.240
asking me how to use Facebook,

00:08:03.240 --> 00:08:05.740
but she didn't have her phone
so I couldn't really help her,

00:08:05.740 --> 00:08:09.470
so she gave me a dollar
anyway and just walked away.

00:08:10.678 --> 00:08:12.050
I don't know, it made me happy,

00:08:12.050 --> 00:08:14.427
that things like that just sort of happen,

00:08:14.427 --> 00:08:16.563
sort of like this right now.

00:08:18.010 --> 00:08:18.843
- The stranger,

00:08:18.843 --> 00:08:20.760
I remember I was about
four or five years old

00:08:20.760 --> 00:08:22.360
and a guy broke into my house,

00:08:22.360 --> 00:08:24.630
he came through my window
and he tried to duck me,

00:08:24.630 --> 00:08:27.310
and then the neighborhood all
jumped on him and tackled him

00:08:27.310 --> 00:08:29.210
and then the cops came and arrested him.

00:08:29.210 --> 00:08:30.900
So that's a stranger that I remember.

00:08:30.900 --> 00:08:33.670
As the story goes I was asleep
during most of the incident.

00:08:33.670 --> 00:08:36.400
- I was in Madrid airport,

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:38.149
I'd been visiting my brother
who was living there,

00:08:38.149 --> 00:08:40.854
and me and my friend were in the queue

00:08:40.854 --> 00:08:45.854
and there was this lady in
front of us, maybe 60 something,

00:08:46.145 --> 00:08:51.145
and she just turned round
and she started to talk to us

00:08:51.260 --> 00:08:52.990
about the power of the universe,

00:08:52.990 --> 00:08:54.540
and "The Secret", which
is actually the first time

00:08:54.540 --> 00:08:55.780
I'd ever heard of that book.

00:08:55.780 --> 00:09:00.601
She was really bizarre, 'cause
she kind of just appeared,

00:09:00.601 --> 00:09:03.170
dropped all this wisdom,

00:09:03.170 --> 00:09:05.100
me and my friends all sat
there and didn't say anything,

00:09:05.100 --> 00:09:06.000
and then she left,

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:08.160
and we actually never saw her again.

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:09.220
And it was really bizarre,

00:09:09.220 --> 00:09:11.580
because we were both questioning
whether it was even real,

00:09:11.580 --> 00:09:13.440
if she was ever there,

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:17.630
but it had a very profound
effect on both of us.

00:09:17.630 --> 00:09:21.140
- We were camping, and
hiking and whatever,

00:09:21.140 --> 00:09:24.410
we had seen this lady
hiking the trail by herself,

00:09:24.410 --> 00:09:25.683
who was maybe in her fifties.

00:09:25.683 --> 00:09:26.860
I thought it was cool,

00:09:26.860 --> 00:09:29.070
I thought if I was single in my fifties,

00:09:29.070 --> 00:09:31.586
I'd still go out and do this cool stuff.

00:09:31.586 --> 00:09:35.338
And it was weird because
when we were camping

00:09:35.338 --> 00:09:38.194
she actually had the camp spot next to us,

00:09:38.194 --> 00:09:40.470
and came over and asked to share our fire,

00:09:40.470 --> 00:09:42.770
so she wouldn't have to
make her own, or whatever.

00:09:42.770 --> 00:09:45.500
I thought it was cool
because, I don't know,

00:09:45.500 --> 00:09:46.460
I have a big family

00:09:46.460 --> 00:09:49.810
and I've never done
anything by myself before,

00:09:49.810 --> 00:09:50.786
and so I was like,

00:09:50.786 --> 00:09:53.310
I'm gonna do that, I can do that,

00:09:53.310 --> 00:09:54.270
I could go hiking,

00:09:54.270 --> 00:09:56.724
I could invite myself
to stranger's campfires

00:09:56.724 --> 00:09:59.287
or something like that, you know?

00:09:59.287 --> 00:10:01.338
- I went to church once, and this lady,

00:10:01.338 --> 00:10:04.240
I was probably ten years
old, sat right next to me.

00:10:04.240 --> 00:10:06.740
Never knew her, never met her,
obviously she's a stranger,

00:10:06.740 --> 00:10:09.650
and she give me a dollar
to give into the basket

00:10:09.650 --> 00:10:11.310
and she just told me that

00:10:11.310 --> 00:10:14.720
she will pray for me for
the rest of her life.

00:10:14.720 --> 00:10:16.860
And that just stuck to me
because I didn't know her,

00:10:16.860 --> 00:10:18.320
and I told her, I'll pray for you as well,

00:10:18.320 --> 00:10:21.830
and I didn't believe in
religion in the moment,

00:10:21.830 --> 00:10:23.270
I still don't but I was like,

00:10:23.270 --> 00:10:25.840
God, if you are real,
take care of this woman.

00:10:25.840 --> 00:10:27.240
- I met her at a camp,

00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:31.094
and we were doing this
exercise of vulnerability,

00:10:31.094 --> 00:10:32.970
and being able to make eye contact

00:10:32.970 --> 00:10:34.580
with somebody you don't know.

00:10:34.580 --> 00:10:36.255
We were in this group of 50 people,

00:10:36.255 --> 00:10:39.740
her and I were moving in the circle,

00:10:39.740 --> 00:10:41.560
moving from person to person,

00:10:41.560 --> 00:10:44.323
and when her and I met eyes,

00:10:44.323 --> 00:10:46.350
we both just started crying.

00:10:46.350 --> 00:10:47.660
At that time I didn't have

00:10:47.660 --> 00:10:49.159
a good relationship with my mother,

00:10:49.159 --> 00:10:52.430
so when I saw her

00:10:52.430 --> 00:10:54.970
I felt like I saw an
older version of my mom,

00:10:54.970 --> 00:10:56.890
and that time I really desired to have

00:10:56.890 --> 00:10:59.840
a better connection, better
relationship with my own mother,

00:10:59.840 --> 00:11:03.550
that's just something that
was going on in my deep self.

00:11:03.550 --> 00:11:05.560
- The stranger was here at the park,

00:11:05.560 --> 00:11:09.891
and he was an older
gentleman that we stopped,

00:11:09.891 --> 00:11:13.008
actually right over here,
to take a picture of us,

00:11:13.008 --> 00:11:14.700
believe it or not.

00:11:14.700 --> 00:11:16.990
And he did a fantastic job,

00:11:16.990 --> 00:11:19.939
he just stopped and asked
all the right questions,

00:11:19.939 --> 00:11:23.000
he was a little eccentric,
to say the least,

00:11:23.000 --> 00:11:25.112
but he did a great job
taking the pictures,

00:11:25.112 --> 00:11:27.020
when I was walking by right now,

00:11:27.020 --> 00:11:28.050
I just happened to remember him,

00:11:28.050 --> 00:11:31.100
you happened to ask that
question, so it's interesting.

00:11:31.100 --> 00:11:36.100
- I was actually looking for
a rock shop close to our area,

00:11:36.460 --> 00:11:40.650
and I found this guy that has
a rock shop in his basement.

00:11:40.650 --> 00:11:43.282
He took me on a hunting trip for flint,

00:11:43.282 --> 00:11:46.610
and then two hour, actually four hour trip

00:11:46.610 --> 00:11:50.593
we found out we were probably
brothers of another mother.

00:11:51.550 --> 00:11:53.818
We became instant best friends,

00:11:53.818 --> 00:11:56.240
so it kind of seems like

00:11:59.300 --> 00:12:04.162
I've never met a stranger
that I didn't like,

00:12:04.162 --> 00:12:06.829
and somehow become best friends.

00:12:08.103 --> 00:12:10.686
(gentle music)

